<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="129" project="customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>customer.bicep: invoke kv-secrets.generated.bicep + write real values from sibling-module outputs (closes task 126's Gap 2 -- partially, see escalations)</title>
    <phase>Phase C'' Wave G-2.5 -- customer.bicep completion (Path 1 owner decision, post-Wave-G-2 discovery)</phase>
    <gate>blocked</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Owner-directed FULL. Also independently justified: this task directly determines whether H4 (task 125/126, already shipped) reports Success or QuarantineRequired on a fresh customer's first provisioning run -- task 126's own deviations report calls this "the single most important finding" blocking Wave G-2 deployability.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Mechanical wiring of one already-generated module (kv-secrets.generated.bicep, task 084) into customer.bicep, with a bounded, enumerable set of secretValues entries sourced from sibling-module outputs already present after tasks 127/128 land. No new Bicep design; the honest-omission discipline (what NOT to fabricate) is the main judgment call, already resolved by this POML's constraints. Sonnet @ high.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <deps>102, 103, 123, 127, 128</deps>
    <tags>bicep-authoring, azure-deployment, deploy</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4-6 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Task 126's deviations report (notes/task-126-deviations.md, Deviation #3, 2026-08-19) documents that KvSecretValueResolver.ResolveAsync ALWAYS returns Failed for `KvSecretValueSource.FromBicepOutput` manifest entries whose secret does not already exist on the target vault, because `scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/generated/kv-secrets.generated.bicep` (task 084) -- the INTENDED writer for these ~15 entries -- is grep-verified referenced only in comments across infrastructure/bicep/, never invoked as an actual `module` call. On a fresh customer this makes H4 report QuarantineRequired (KvWritePartialFailure) for roughly 15 of manifest.yaml's 26 canonical secrets. Task 126's own recommended follow-on (a): "a new task wires kv-secrets.generated.bicep into the stack bicep files" is exactly this task's mandate, scoped to customer.bicep only (the sole template task 123's ArmDeploymentRunner deploys in production -- do NOT touch stacks/model1-shared.bicep or stacks/model2-full.bicep, out of scope). IMPORTANT CORRECTION to the mechanism: H4's SecretClientKvWriter does NOT read ARM deployment `outputs` JSON for these entries -- it checks secret EXISTENCE directly on the target Key Vault via SecretClient and skips the resolver entirely when a FromBicepOutput secret already exists (task 126 deviations, Deviation #3: "the writer skips calling the resolver at all when the secret DOES already exist"). "Threading outputs to H4" therefore means: this Bicep composition WRITES the real secret values ONTO THE VAULT during the ARM deploy (kv-secrets.generated.bicep's actual job), so that by the time H4 runs, those secrets already exist and H4 no-ops/succeeds on them instead of failing. This task does NOT modify any C# and does NOT add a new InterStepState field.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer. Expert in Bicep module composition and honest-signal discipline (never fabricate a value the composition cannot actually resolve).</role>

  <goal>customer.bicep invokes the existing modules/../scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/generated/kv-secrets.generated.bicep module AFTER all resource modules it depends on (including task 127's UAMI/App Service and task 128's OpenAI/AI Search), passing a `secretValues` object populated ONLY with the canonical KV secret names this Bicep composition can genuinely resolve from its own sibling-module outputs. Every canonical name this composition cannot resolve is deliberately OMITTED from `secretValues` (never fabricated with a placeholder) and documented inline + in this task's completion notes with the specific reason and a recommended owner, so the residual gap is visible and actionable rather than silently narrowed.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Task 126 (H4 real value-sourcing, completed 2026-08-19) fixed the OTHER three KvSecretValueSource branches (Generated / FromExistingKvSecret / FromRunParameters) to write real values instead of the literal `{name}-interim-placeholder-{customerId}` string, but explicitly could NOT fix FromBicepOutput within its own declared scope (KvSecretValueResolver.cs + the manifest DI-swap) because the actual value-writer for those entries is a Bicep module, not C#. This task is the Bicep-side completion task 126 flagged as necessary and did not build.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep</file>
      <file role="modify">infrastructure/bicep/customer.json (regenerate via az bicep build --outfile)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/generated/kv-secrets.generated.bicep (task 084 -- DO NOT MODIFY; DO NOT hand-edit, it is a generated file with a "DO NOT EDIT BY HAND" banner; if it needs to change, that is a manifest/generator change, out of this task's scope)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml (source of truth for each canonical secret's `value_source` classification + purpose)</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/notes/task-126-deviations.md (Deviation #3 -- the exact ~15-entry list + the recommended follow-on paths (a)/(b)/(c) this task implements path (a))</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/server/services/Sprk.Provisioning.ControlPlane.Core/Handlers/KvSecretsPopulation/SecretClientKvWriter.cs (read-only, to confirm the exists-check-skips-resolver mechanism cited in this task's prompt; do NOT modify)</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="102" status="pending">wave-sequencing</dependency>
      <dependency task="103" status="pending">DI plumbing context (informational -- this task is Bicep-only, no C#)</dependency>
      <dependency task="123" status="pending">H2a is the ARM deployment consumer this composition serves</dependency>
      <dependency task="127" status="pending">Provides `uami`/`bffApi`/`bffApiSlot` module outputs this task's secretValues object does NOT directly need, but the App Service section this task's module invocation is placed AFTER must already exist in the file</dependency>
      <dependency task="128" status="pending">Provides `openAi.outputs.openAiKey` + `aiSearch.outputs.searchServiceAdminKey` + endpoints -- REQUIRED inputs to this task's secretValues object. This task MUST execute after 127 and 128 both land (not parallel-safe with either).</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11 (Component Justification -- Default to Reuse)">kv-secrets.generated.bicep already exists (task 084) and is the single canonical KV-secret-declaration source per its own header comment ("Consumers (task 086): reference this module from customer.bicep... instead of declaring KV secrets inline"). MUST invoke it, unmodified, as a module. MUST NOT declare any `Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults/secrets` resource inline in customer.bicep -- that would create a second, competing source of truth for secret declarations (exactly what task 084/086 exist to prevent).</constraint>
    <constraint source="task-126-deviations.md Deviation #3 (mechanism correction, binding for this task)">H4 does NOT read ARM `outputs` JSON for FromBicepOutput entries -- it checks secret EXISTENCE on the vault via SecretClient. This task's job is to make the secret EXIST (with a real value) on the vault by deploy time, not to add a new ARM output or InterStepState field. Do not build an output-threading mechanism that doesn't correspond to how H4 actually reads these values.</constraint>
    <constraint source="Honest-signal discipline (root CLAUDE.md §6.5 anti-silent-compliance + operator's own standing rule to fix drift at discovery, not defer it)">For every one of manifest.yaml's `FromBicepOutput`-classified canonical names that this Bicep composition CANNOT genuinely resolve (no sibling-module output exists to supply it), the name MUST be OMITTED entirely from the `secretValues` object -- never populated with an invented, synthetic, or placeholder-looking value. kv-secrets.generated.bicep already handles an absent key via its own `contains(secretValues, ...)` check + `placeholderValue` fallback; leaning on that existing mechanism (rather than writing a fake value from this task) is the CORRECT way to keep the signal honest for whichever downstream mechanism eventually resolves it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="task-126-deviations.md Deviation #3 -- exact 15-entry list to triage">AiSearch--AdminKey, AiSearch-Endpoint, AppInsights-ConnectionString, AzureOpenAI-Endpoint, BFF-API-Audience, BFF-API-ClientId, Communication-WebhookUrl, DocumentIntelligence-ApiKey, DocumentIntelligence-Endpoint, Redis-ConnectionString, ServiceBus-ConnectionString, SPE-CommunicationArchiveContainerId, SPE-ContainerTypeId, SPE-DefaultContainerId, Storage-ConnectionString. This task MUST triage each one individually (resolve vs. deliberately omit) rather than treating the list as all-or-nothing.</constraint>
    <constraint source="Scope boundary vs. task 127/128 (do not silently expand)">Do NOT wire modules/doc-intelligence.bicep or modules/monitoring.bicep into customer.bicep to resolve DocumentIntelligence-*/AppInsights-ConnectionString -- those two resources are OUTSIDE 127/128's declared scope (flagged as their own escalation trigger in both those POMLs) and are therefore outside this task's scope too. Omit those entries; do not opportunistically add the missing resources as a side effect of this task.</constraint>
    <constraint source="Redis architecture conflict (do not silently resolve)">Redis-ConnectionString's `value_source: FromBicepOutput` classification in manifest.yaml directly conflicts with Q-E FR-12 / design.md §7 (Redis is per-ENVIRONMENT, not per-customer -- customer.bicep deliberately does NOT provision Redis). This composition has no Redis module output to supply. Omit this entry; do NOT invent a cross-reference to the per-env Redis KV secret from within the per-customer stamp (that would require a cross-resource-group / cross-vault reference this module was not designed for) -- surface as an escalation trigger instead (below).</constraint>
    <constraint source="BFF-API-ClientId / BFF-API-Audience classification concern (do not silently resolve)">spec.md's MUST rules assign per-customer BFF Entra app-registration creation to H3 (task 130, Graph app-reg port -- a C# runtime handler), not to Bicep. manifest.yaml classifies these two as `FromBicepOutput`, which appears to be a manifest-classification mismatch (they look like H3 run-parameter / InterStepState outputs, not ARM deployment outputs). This composition has no Entra app-registration resource and MUST NOT create one (Microsoft Graph/AzAPI resource creation in subscription-scope Bicep is out of scope and would duplicate H3's declared ownership). Omit these two entries; surface as an escalation trigger instead (below).</constraint>
    <constraint source="secretValues param is @secure() -- do not weaken">The `secretValues` object parameter on kv-secrets.generated.bicep is already `@secure()`. Do not add a plaintext top-level customer.bicep output that echoes any of these values (customer.bicep's existing secret-bearing outputs like `storageConnectionString` are pre-existing and already carry the `#disable-next-line outputs-should-not-contain-secrets` marker -- do not add NEW ones for this task's additions).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>customer.bicep kv-secrets.generated.bicep invocation (Gap 2)</topic>
    <files>
      <file>projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/design.md §7.7 (Key Vault Secrets populated by H4 -- canonical names)</file>
      <file>projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/CLAUDE.md</file>
      <file>projects/customer-provisioning-orchestration-r1/notes/task-126-deviations.md</file>
      <file>scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor + load">Emit RIGOR LEVEL (FULL). Read customer.bicep as it stands after tasks 127+128 land, kv-secrets.generated.bicep in full (params + the 26 resource blocks), manifest.yaml's `value_source` field for each of the 15 FromBicepOutput-classified entries, and task-126-deviations.md Deviation #3 in full.</step>
    <step order="1" name="Triage the 15 entries">For each of the 15 entries in this POML's constraints list, determine: (a) resolvable from an existing customer.bicep sibling-module output (post-127/128) -- list the exact expression; or (b) not resolvable -- list the specific blocking reason (resource not provisioned / architecture conflict / manifest-classification mismatch / runtime-only value). Expect roughly 6 resolvable (AiSearch--AdminKey, AiSearch-Endpoint, AzureOpenAI-Endpoint, ServiceBus-ConnectionString, Storage-ConnectionString, Communication-WebhookUrl) and 9 not resolvable (the 3 SPE-* entries -- H8/H9 runtime outputs, not ARM deploy-time; Redis-ConnectionString -- per-env architecture conflict; DocumentIntelligence-ApiKey/Endpoint + AppInsights-ConnectionString -- resource not wired, out of 127/128 scope; BFF-API-ClientId/BFF-API-Audience -- H3 runtime output, manifest-classification concern). Do not treat this expected split as a target to force -- verify each independently against the actual post-127/128 customer.bicep outputs.</step>
    <step order="2" name="Build secretValues object">Add a `var kvSecretValues = { ... }` (or equivalent) populated ONLY with the resolvable entries from step 1, each value a direct reference to an existing sibling-module output (e.g. `aiSearch.outputs.searchServiceAdminKey`, `openAi.outputs.openAiEndpoint`, `serviceBus.outputs.serviceBusConnectionString`, `storage.outputs.connectionString`, and a constructed `'https://${bffApi.outputs.appServiceUrl}/...'`-style URL for Communication-WebhookUrl matching the pattern already proven in stacks/model2-full.bicep line 243). Add a `// NOT resolvable: &lt;reason&gt;` comment for each of the 9 omitted canonical names, immediately above or below the var declaration, so a future task author sees the omission without re-deriving it.</step>
    <step order="3" name="Invoke the module">Add `module kvSecrets 'scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/generated/kv-secrets.generated.bicep'` (verify the correct relative path from infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep) with `scope: rg`, `params: { keyVaultName: keyVaultName, secretValues: kvSecretValues }`, placed AFTER every module it references in `dependsOn` (or relies on implicit Bicep dependency resolution via the symbolic references in kvSecretValues -- prefer implicit resolution, only add explicit `dependsOn` if Bicep's compiler flags a missing edge).</step>
    <step order="4" name="Validate">`az bicep build --file infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep --outfile infrastructure/bicep/customer.json` -- 0 errors, 0 warnings. Commit the regenerated customer.json.</step>
    <step order="5" name="What-if (or documented deferral)">If live Azure reachability is available, run `az deployment sub what-if` against a target vault and confirm the 6 resolvable secrets show as Create/Update with non-placeholder values (do not print the actual secret values in any log/note). If not reachable, document as deferred, per precedent.</step>
    <step order="6" name="Manifest.yaml fix per owner E3 (2026-08-19)">Owner confirmed 2026-08-19 (via AskUserQuestion) that BFF-API-ClientId + BFF-API-Audience are manifest-misclassified. Fix: edit `scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml` to reclassify these two entries from `FromBicepOutput` to `FromRunParameters` (H3 creates the BFF app-reg at runtime and writes ClientId/Audience to RunParameters.Secrets per the task-126 real-value-resolver contract). Add an inline comment on each entry citing "H3 (task 130) is the value producer; DAG runs H3 before H4". Then invoke the canonical-secret-catalog GENERATOR (find via `find scripts/canonical-secret-catalog -type f -name "*.ps1"` or similar — likely `Generate-*.ps1`) to regenerate `kv-secrets.generated.bicep` from the updated manifest. Verify: the generated .bicep no longer references BFF-API-ClientId / BFF-API-Audience in the FromBicepOutput block (they now flow through H4's FromRunParameters path at runtime). This removes those 2 entries from this task's "omitted" list — they now succeed at runtime via H4 (task 126's FromRunParameters resolver), not via this Bicep composition.</step>
    <step order="7" name="Completion notes -- residual gap">Document the FINAL split (post-128b + this task's manifest fix). Expected shape:
    - Now RESOLVED via 128b's newly-wired outputs: Redis-ConnectionString (from `redis.outputs.redisConnectionString` — owner E2 confirmation, 128b landed the Redis module), DocumentIntelligence-ApiKey (from `docIntelligence.outputs.docIntelligenceKey` — 128b landed the DocIntel module), DocumentIntelligence-Endpoint, AppInsights-ConnectionString (from monitoring.outputs.connectionString — 128b landed the monitoring module).
    - Now RESOLVED via manifest.yaml reclassification (step 6): BFF-API-ClientId, BFF-API-Audience (H3 runtime via FromRunParameters).
    - Still NOT resolvable at Bicep-deploy-time (H8/H9 runtime outputs; expected to succeed at runtime via H4's FromRunParameters after their handlers run): SPE-ContainerTypeId, SPE-DefaultContainerId, SPE-CommunicationArchiveContainerId. Document these as expected-runtime-resolution, not permanent failures.
    - State explicitly: this task (combined with task 128b's completion) REDUCES the task-126-deviations "QuarantineRequired on fresh customer" risk from ~15 failing FromBicepOutput entries to 0 permanent Bicep-side failures. All previously-omitted entries now flow through either Bicep (11 entries — 6 original + Redis + 3 DocIntel/AppInsights + [none since BFF moved to FromRunParameters]) or H4's runtime FromRunParameters path (5 entries — 3 SPE-* from H8/H9 + BFF-ClientId + BFF-Audience from H3).</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <!-- RESOLVED 2026-08-19 via AskUserQuestion + task 128b Path B: E2 (Redis) resolved by 128b wiring Redis in customer.bicep (owner confirmed Model 2 per-customer semantics); E3 (BFF-API-*) resolved by step 6 manifest.yaml reclassification. Trigger 1 and 2 below preserved for historical context on the reasoning; the executor does NOT need to re-STOP-and-confirm. Trigger 3 remains live. -->
    <trigger>[RESOLVED — see note above] Redis-ConnectionString's `FromBicepOutput` classification conflicts with the per-environment (not per-customer) Redis architecture (Q-E FR-12). STOP and surface to the project owner per CLAUDE.md §6.5: either (a) the manifest entry should be reclassified (e.g. to a value H4 skips entirely for Model2Dedicated, or resolves via a documented cross-vault read of the per-env Redis secret), or (b) this entry permanently triggers QuarantineRequired and the project accepts that as expected behavior with an operator runbook step to populate it out-of-band. Do not pick a path unilaterally in this task.</trigger>
    <trigger>[RESOLVED — see note above] BFF-API-ClientId / BFF-API-Audience appear manifest-misclassified as `FromBicepOutput` when spec.md assigns their creation to H3 (a C# runtime handler, task 130), not Bicep. STOP and escalate to the canonical-secret-catalog manifest owner (task-084 lineage) for a manifest.yaml correction (likely `FromRunParameters` sourced from H3's InterStepState, not `FromBicepOutput`) rather than fabricating a Bicep-side Entra app-registration resource to satisfy the current (likely wrong) classification.</trigger>
    <trigger>If, after step 1's triage, MORE than the expected ~9 entries turn out unresolvable (e.g. task 127 or 128 did not land the outputs this task assumes), STOP and confirm those tasks' actual shipped output names before proceeding -- do not adapt by inventing a workaround output.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <tools>
    <tool name="az bicep">build</tool>
    <tool name="az deployment">sub what-if (if reachable)</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="infra">infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep (modified)</output>
    <output type="infra">infrastructure/bicep/customer.json (regenerated)</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">az bicep build infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep exits 0 with 0 warnings.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">grep 'kv-secrets.generated.bicep' infrastructure/bicep/customer.bicep shows a module invocation (not a comment reference).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The secretValues object literal contains AT LEAST these 6 always-resolvable keys: AiSearch--AdminKey, AiSearch-Endpoint, AzureOpenAI-Endpoint, ServiceBus-ConnectionString, Storage-ConnectionString, Communication-WebhookUrl. AFTER task 128b lands (owner E2 + amendment) it MUST ALSO contain Redis-ConnectionString, DocumentIntelligence-ApiKey, DocumentIntelligence-Endpoint, AppInsights-ConnectionString (4 new resolvables from 128b's Redis + DocIntel + monitoring modules) — verify via grep before finalizing this task.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The secretValues object literal does NOT contain any of these 5 permanently-not-Bicep-resolvable keys: SPE-ContainerTypeId, SPE-DefaultContainerId, SPE-CommunicationArchiveContainerId (H8/H9 runtime), BFF-API-ClientId, BFF-API-Audience (H3 runtime, now correctly classified FromRunParameters via step 6 manifest fix).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Step 6 manifest fix landed: `grep "value_source: FromRunParameters" scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml` returns matches for BOTH BFF-API-ClientId and BFF-API-Audience entries. `grep "value_source: FromBicepOutput" scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/manifest.yaml` no longer returns those two entries. `kv-secrets.generated.bicep` was regenerated (git diff shows it) and its regeneration reflects the manifest change.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">scripts/canonical-secret-catalog/generated/kv-secrets.generated.bicep is NOT modified by this task (git diff on that file is empty).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No new plaintext top-level output in customer.bicep echoes any secretValues entry.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">infrastructure/bicep/customer.json is regenerated and committed alongside customer.bicep.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The task's completion notes enumerate all 9 omitted entries with a specific reason + recommended owner each.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="false">az deployment sub what-if (when reachable) confirms the 6 resolvable secrets land on the target vault with real values -- OR explicitly documented as deferred to a live ceremony.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Justification (CLAUDE.md §11): Existing -- kv-secrets.generated.bicep already exists (task 084) as the single canonical KV-secret-declaration source; it is simply never invoked. Extension -- invoking an already-generated module with a values object sourced from already-existing sibling-module outputs is the minimal correct move; no new secret-declaration mechanism is introduced. Cost-of-doing-nothing -- H4 (task 125/126, already shipped and otherwise correct) reports QuarantineRequired on every fresh Model2Dedicated customer's first provisioning run for ~15 of 26 canonical secrets, per task 126's own deviations report calling this "the single most important finding... needs owner/orchestrator attention before Wave G-2 is considered deployable."</notes>

  <execution>
    <skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill>
  </execution>
</task>
